  "query_preview": "(This Evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 221 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias will move away to the east with \nupper level ridging set to build in immediately for the early part \nof the week. This will set the stage for near record breaking warmth \nMonday and Tuesday. A frontal boundary will move into the area for \nmidweek with periodic rain and storms Tuesday night through \nThursday. The coolest air of the season so far will follow for next \nweekend.\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Tuesday \n\nThe back edge of the stratus deck continues to make slow but steady \neastward progress across central Indiana early this afternoon. Skies \nwere mostly sunny in the Wabash Valley with thicker clouds largely \nconfined to areas east of Interstate 69. Temperatures varied widely \nas a result of the clouds...ranging from the lower 60 in eastern \ncounties to the mid 70s in the lower Wabash Valley.\n\nThe departure of the stratus associated with what is left of Isaias \nwill be followed by high pressure and mainly clear skies tonight. \nWith winds becoming near calm...potential exists for patchy fog \nformation in the predawn hours through daybreak Monday focused \nprimarily over the eastern half of the forecast area. The fog will \nburn off quickly Monday morning as boundary layer mixing commences.\n\nThe arrival and expansion of a broad upper level ridge into the Ohio \nValley on Monday will signal an abrupt transition...and the first of \nseveral upcoming over the next seven days...as stronger southwest \nflow develops between a frontal boundary over the upper Midwest and \nthe aforementioned high pressure as it settles along the Gulf coast. \nStrong warm advection will commence and result in well above normal \ntemperatures for Monday and Tuesday across the region. Highs may \nflirt with 90 degrees in the Wabash Valley both days. For KIND... \nrecord highs of 86 for both Monday and Tuesday will be threatened. \nThe frontal boundary over the Great Lakes will drift south towards \nthe area late Tuesday but any convection is expected to remain \nto the north through Tuesday evening.\n\nTuesday Night Through Sunday\n\nAn increasingly amplified flow aloft driven first by an upper low \nover the Rockies and then more significantly by a deepening northern \nstream trough will flatten the ridge over the Gulf coast while \ninitiating a substantial pattern change for the region late week \ninto next weekend. The aforementioned boundary will remain in a \nquasi-stationary state over the Ohio Valley midweek before lifting \nback north Thursday in response to a surface low ejecting out of the \nRockies and increased upper jet phasing. Rain with embedded \nthunderstorms will increase in coverage across the region as a \nresult Wednesday and Thursday as multiple surface waves ride along \nthe boundary. There remains at least some potential for a few \nstronger storms...especially ahead of a strong cold front Thursday \nafternoon but the potential for an abundance of clouds and rainfall \nposes some uncertainty in this prospect. With a deep moisture plume \npooling along the front aided in part from remnant moisture from the \ntwo eastern Pacific hurricanes...there is the possibility for some \nlocations to see one to two inches by Thursday night.\n\nThe cold front will sweep across the region Thursday night and early \nFriday. In the wake of the frontal passage...the aforementioned \nupper trough will settle and amplify across much of the eastern half \nof the country. A much cooler airmass will overspread the region \nwith strong cold advection in place. Highs primarily in the 60s on \nFriday will remain in the 50s Saturday making for the coolest \ndaytime temperatures experienced in over 5 months. While a lack of \nboundary layer decoupling may limit frost accrual to sheltered \nlocales Friday night...confidence continues to grow in a widespread \nfrost for much of the area on Saturday night as lows fall into the \n30s. Subfreezing temperatures are squarely on the table for some of \nour normal cool spots as well Saturday night.",
